17:Autism spectrum disorder ( ' autism ' ) is a neurodevelopmental condition characterized by substantial behavioral and neuroanatomical heterogeneity. This poses signi /uniFB01 cant challenges to understanding its neurobiological mechanisms and developing effective interventions. Identifying phenotypically more homogeneous subgroups and shifting the focus from average group differences to individuals is a promising approach to addressing heterogeneity. In the present study, we aimed to parse clinical and neuroanatomical heterogeneity in autism by combining clustering of clinical features with normative modeling based on neuroanatomical measures (cortical thickness [CT] and subcortical volume) within the Autism Brain Imaging Data Exchange data sets (N autism = 861, N nonautistic individuals [N NAI] = 886, age range = 5 -64). First, model-based clustering was applied to autistic symptoms as measured by the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule to identify clinical subgroups of autism (N autism = 499). Next, we ran normative modeling on CT and subcortical parcellations (N autism = 690, N NAI = 744) and examined whether clinical subgrouping resulted in increased neurobiological homogeneity within the subgroups compared to the entire autism group by comparing their spatial overlap of neuroanatomical deviations. We further investigated whether the identi /uniFB01 ed subgroups improved the accuracy of autism classi /uniFB01 cation based on the neuroanatomical deviations using supervised machine learning with cross-validation. Results yielded two clinical subgroups primarily differing in restrictive and repetitive behaviors (RRBs). Both subgroups showed increased homogeneity in localized deviations with the high-RRB subgroup showing increased volume deviations in the cerebellum and the low-RRB subgroup showing decreased CT deviations predominantly in the postcentral gyrus and fusiform cortex. Nevertheless, substantial within-group heterogeneity remained highlighted by the lack of improvement of the classi /uniFB01 er ' s performance when distinguishing between the subgroups and NAI. Future research should aim to further reduce heterogeneity incorporating additional neuroanatomical clustering in even larger samples. This will eventually pave the way for more tailored behavioral interventions and improving clinical outcomes.
37:Keywords: autism, cortical thickness, heterogeneity, subgrouping, normative modeling
43:An increasingly popular strategy for addressing clinical heterogeneity involves the delineation of subgroups to foster greater group homogeneity (Lai et al., 2013). The identi /uniFB01 cation of clinically meaningful subgroups is crucial for targeted support, particularly considering the current prominence of behavioral programs as the most ef /uniFB01 cacious mode of intervention (Baribeau et al., 2022). First efforts have employed clustering algorithms to identify subgroups with different clinical feature compositions (Bitsika et al., 2008; Cholemkery et al., 2016; Georgiades et al., 2013; Hu &amp; Steinberg, 2009; Hus et al., 2007; Lefort-Besnard et al., 2020; Lombardo et al., 2016; Mandelli et al., 2023). These studies have utilized clinical measures, such as core clinical features (e.g., Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule [ADOS], Autism Diagnostic Interview [ADI]), adaptive functioning, or higher-order cognitive domains to identify between three to /uniFB01 ve subgroups which were characterized by different symptom pro /uniFB01 les. However, among these, some studies show that identi/uniFB01 ed subgroups may most strongly differ by demographic variables such as age, full-scale intelligence quotient (FIQ), and sex (Bitsika et al., 2008; Lefort-Besnard et al., 2020). These intertwined associations between these subgroups and demographic variables introduce ambiguity as to whether the observed subgroup variations stem from clinically relevant metrics or basic demographic measures. Furthermore, the studies investigating autism subgroups did not further examine whether these clinically differentiated subgroups might also yield heightened homogeneity in brain structure. Such homogeneity in brain structure could potentially provide insights into the speci /uniFB01 c neuroanatomical foundations underlying distinct autistic traits.
47:Apromising novel method for addressing the neurobiological heterogeneity in mental health conditions is normative modeling (Marquand et al., 2016; Rutherford et al., 2022). A normative model captures the trajectory of the typical developing brain across relevant variables such as age, which can be used to predict CT or volume for each individual and thereby show the deviation from the typical pattern for each individual. Previous studies used normative modeling to identify individualized deviations of CT among autistic individuals, revealing heterogeneous widespread patterns of deviations (Bethlehem et al., 2020; Segal et al., 2023; Zabihi et al., 2019, 2020). Clustering at the neurobiological level has further been used on the basis of deviations derived from normative models (Bethlehem et al., 2020; Shan et al., 2022; Zabihi et al., 2020). Clustering at the clinical level in combination with normative models has been used in prede /uniFB01 ned language subgroups only (Floris et al., 2021). However, no previous studies have used normative model-derived neuroanatomical measures to characterize datadriven clinical subgroups.
49:In the present study, we aimed to investigate whether reduction in clinical heterogeneity would lead to a reduction in neurotomical heterogeneity (i.e., greater homogeneity) in autism. For this, we leveraged the large-scale, publicly available Autism Brain Imaging Data Exchange (ABIDE) data sets (Di Martino et al., 2014, 2017) and employed three analytical approaches: /uniFB01 rst, we used clustering to identify distinct subgroups within autism that differed in their clinical symptom pro /uniFB01 les. Next, we characterized the similarity (i.e., homogeneity) of normative modeling-derived neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 les within the previously identi /uniFB01 ed subgroups. Finally, we tested a support vector machine (SVM)-based classi /uniFB01 er ' s ability to distinguish between the clinical subgroups from NAI based on their neuroanatomical features. Based on our hypothesis, we expected (a) higher similarity (i.e., homogeneity) in CT and volume deviations in the clinical subgroups compared to the entire autism group and (b) an improved classi /uniFB01 cation
59:For primary analyses, we excluded females due to scarce availability and autistic individuals whose ADOS-Generic (ADOS-G) had not been administered by research-reliable personnel (N autism = 69). For the clustering and normative modeling analyses different sets of inclusion criteria were applied which resulted in differently sized samples (see Figure S1 in the online supplemental materials). Speci /uniFB01 cally, for the clustering analysis (based on clinical data, i.e., ADOS-G), we excluded autistic participants with missing ADOS-G scores (N = 348) and FIQ (N = 55) resulting in a sample of 499 autistic individuals. For the normative modeling analysis (based on neuroimaging data), participants with missing ADOS-G scores were retained to increase sample size, however, we applied the following additional exclusion criteria: (a) participants with lacking neuroimaging data (N autism = 71, N NAI = 37); (b) low image quality as assessed by both visual inspection and the Euler number (N autism = 15, N NAI = 7). The Euler number is related to the number of holes in the reconstructed surface and represents its complexity (Dale et al., 1999). In line with previous reports that also utilized ABIDE I and ABIDE II (Bethlehem et al., 2020), we excluded individuals with values smaller than -300; (c) corrupted preprocessing (N autism = 4, N NAI = 1); (d) participants missing FIQ information (N autism = 47, N NAI = 66); and (e) sites with less than 10 individuals per diagnostic group (Bayer et al., 2022; Gaiser et al., 2023) (N autism = 69, N NAI = 15). For further details see the online supplemental materials. This resulted in a sample of 690 autistic and 744 NAI between 5 and 64 years who were included in the normative modeling analysis (see Table 1). A /uniFB02 owchart depicting the selection process for each analytical step is visualized in Figure S1 in the online supplemental materials.
61:Given that large sample sizes are necessary for robust (sexstrati /uniFB01 ed) clustering and normative modeling analyses, primary
71:Note. The sample size re /uniFB02 ects the total number of individuals before applying analyses-speci /uniFB01 c exclusion criteria for clustering and normative modeling. Differences between autism and NAI in age and FIQ were determined with a Wilcoxon signed rank test. NAI = nonautistic individuals; FIQ = full-scale intelligence quotient.
77:We aimed to identify clinically distinct subgroups in autism using the ADOS-G (Lord et al., 2000) subscores for social interaction, communication, and RRBs. After z -standardizing the subscores, we additionally accounted for confounding variables, including age, site, FIQ, ADOS-Gmodule, and the other two subscores using a General Linear Model. The resulting residuals were used for the subsequent analyses.
79:Next, we employed a model-based clustering method (Banerjee &amp; Shan, 2010) to identify clinical subgroups in autistic individuals. The density estimation was based on parameterized /uniFB01 nite Gaussian mixture models estimated by expectation-maximization algorithm initialized by hierarchical model-based agglomerative clustering. The optimal number of clusters was determined according to the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC). To assess the stability of the clustering results, we performed bootstrapping with 10.000 iterations and computed the Jaccard Similarity Index (JI) (Hennig, 2007). Finally, we tested for differences in subscores between the identi /uniFB01 ed subgroups using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. To avoid in /uniFB02 ation of the Type I error rate (Gao et al., 2024), we additionally used the ' clusterpval ' package in RStudio with 10.000 draws to determine the signi /uniFB01 cant differences in means between the clusters (Gao et al., 2024; Winter &amp; Hahn, 2022). For further details, see the online supplemental materials.
81:## Image Processing
83:We processed the T1-weighted images from the ABIDE datasets using the FreeSurfer software (Version 7.3.2) (Fischl, 2012). In brief, the FreeSurfer pipeline consists of intensity normalization, skull stripping, and gray matter (GM) and white matter (WM) surface segmentation, resulting in a tessellated representation of the GM/WM boundary. The resulting surfaces were corrected for topological abnormalities and registered to a spherical atlas based on individual cortical folding patterns. CT was extracted for each individual using the Destrieux parcellation scheme consisting of 74 regions in each hemisphere (Destrieux et al., 2010). To label subcortical tissue classes, we further applied the volume-based stream. After af /uniFB01 ne registration with MNI305 space, initial volumetric labeling, B1 bias /uniFB01 eld intensity correction, a high dimensional nonlinear volumetric alignment to the MNI305 atlas was performed and the volumewas labeled (Fischl et al., 2002). For more detailed information, see the online supplemental materials.
87:Normative modeling is a novel statistical framework that allows to compare individual-level neuroanatomical features (CT and subcortical volume) to a reference population (Marquand et al., 2016, 2019; Rutherford et al., 2022, 2023). This method yields coherent measures of predictive con /uniFB01 dence in addition to point estimates, meaning we can predict both the expected neuroanatomical changes and the associated predictive uncertainty for each individual allowing us to quantify the regional deviation of CT and subcortical volume from the neurotypical range.
89:Here, we performed normative modeling on CT and subcortical regions of interests (ROIs) using warped likelihood Bayesian Linear Regression (Fraza et al., 2021) model implemented in the PCNtoolkit (Version 0.26) in Python (Version 3.9.16). We included age, site, FIQ, and quality measures (i.e., the Euler number) as covariates and additional intracranial volume for subcortical volumes. The data set was split into a training set consisting of 90% of NAI and a test set consisting of all autistic individuals and the remaining 10% of NAI. We ensured that age, FIQ, and site were evenly distributed across the training and test sets. A separate normative model was built for each of the 148 CT ROIs and 23 subcortical ROIs independently. Figure S3 in the online supplemental materials depicts an example of a centile map and the model ' s trajectory for the left occipital pole. Next, we computed normative probability maps (NPMs) consisting of subject-speci /uniFB01 c Z -scores, which quantify the deviation of each participant from the normative model in each ROI. To evaluate model performance, we obtained state-of-the-art model evaluation parameters (see Figure S4 in the online supplemental materials).
93:To visually illustrate the heterogeneity in autism, we depict the individual NPMs for the top 10 participants with the highest deviation scores (see Figure S5 in the online supplemental materials). To further statistically characterize heterogeneity, NPMs were thresholded at an absolute value of Z . |2.6| (i.e., p , .005) (Floris et al., 2021; Segal et al., 2023; Wolfers et al., 2018, 2020) and we de /uniFB01 ned extreme positive and extreme negative deviations for each participant in each anatomical location. Here, a positive deviation score indicated higher CT (subcortical) values compared to the reference model. To assess whether subgrouping increases biological homogeneity in autism, we generated spatial overlap maps separately for positive and negative deviations using the percentage of individuals that showed an extreme deviation in each ROI. These overlap maps depict regions where participants shared common deviations from the normative trajectory in each anatomic location in each group (see Figure 2). This means that the more individuals have an extreme deviation from the normative model within the same anatomical location, the greater is neuroanatomical homogeneity expressed in a common neural signature. Thus, the more individuals (within each group) deviate from the normative trajectory within the same anatomical location, the more homogenous their associated neuroanatomy is. In the following, we refer to ' homogeneity/heterogeneity ' when referring to the underlying principle and ' overlap ' when referring to the statistical approach of quantifying homogeneity/heterogeneity. Next, we statistically tested for differences in these overlap maps between the two autistic subgroups and between the entire autism group using group-based permutation tests and subsequent false discovery rate (FDR) correction of p values (Segal et al., 2023).
95:Further, to test the robustness of the results, we conducted an additional sensitivity analysis by controlling the FDR separately for each NPM. For further details see the online supplemental materials. Finally, we assessed biological separability of the subgroups by applying an SVM. We used the deviation Z -scores derived from the normative model for all ROIs as features and evaluated under /uniFB01 vefold cross-validation whether classifying the subgroups from NAI would outperform classifying the entire autism group from NAI (see the online supplemental materials).
103:Using model-based clustering based on ADOS-G subscores, the BIC identi /uniFB01 ed the EEV model (i.e., the best cluster representation was equal in volume [E], equal in shape [E] and varies in orientation [V] between clusters), as best/uniFB01 tting Gaussian model and two as the optimal cluster number (see Figure 1A). This cluster number was stable (JI high-RRB subgroup = 0.78; JI low-RRB subgroup = 0.92). The clusterpval package showed that the two subgroups signi /uniFB01 cantly differed across the core clinical ADOS-subscores (Euclidean distance = 1.430, p = .027). Speci /uniFB01 cally, the two clinical subgroups were primarily distinguished by differences in RRBs ( W = 42,752, p , .001) and to a lesser extent by communication scores ( W = 19,396, p = .02), as depicted in Figure 1B and Table 2. Speci /uniFB01 cally, Subgroup 1 showed signi /uniFB01 cantly higher RRBs, while Subgroup 2 demonstrated signi /uniFB01 cantly more communication dif /uniFB01 culties (Table 2). In the following, we will refer to Subgroup 1 as the high-RRB subgroup and Subgroup 2 as the low-RRB subgroup. The subgroups did not differ in age, FIQ, Euler number or ADOS-G module (see Figure S2 in the online supplemental materials and Table 2). Also, there were no signi /uniFB01 cant correlations between the Euler number and RRB scores ( r =-.02, p = .73), social scores ( r = .06, p = .21), and communication scores ( r =-.02, p = .59). Given that identi /uniFB01 ed subgroups differed in symptom severity on RRBs and communication subscores, we compared our datadriven, unsupervised clustering approach with a hypothesis-driven method (i.e., median-split) to identify subgroups of individuals with high and low symptom severity. This resulted in little overlap of allocated individuals to different subgroups (see the online supplemental materials). For further details and for results in both males and females, see Table S1 and Figure S7 in the online supplemental materials.
